WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.584
SARSA asks: what action will I actually take next?

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.733
It samples that action and bootstraps from it — on-policy.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.267
Q-learning swaps one symbol: instead of sampling, it takes the max.

4
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.947
The target is always greedy. The behavior still explores.

5
00:00:21.027 --> 00:00:24.035
That single swap makes Q-learning off-policy.

6
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.136
Five rows, five columns — five hundred states.

7
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.499
Six actions: four directions, Pickup, and Dropoff.

8
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.459
Every step costs one. An illegal pickup or dropoff costs ten.

9
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.968
Legal dropoff: plus twenty — the only terminal reward.

10
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.752
The task has four sub-goals chained together.

11
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.501
Get to the passenger. Pick them up. Get to the destination. Drop them off.

12
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.797
Q-learning will compose all four — with no explicit plan.

13
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.757
State sixteen: taxi at R, passenger aboard, destination R.

14
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.160
The agent executes Dropoff. The Q-table starts learning.

15
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.797
The Q-learning update — TD target times learning rate.

16
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.603
Terminal step: the bootstrap is zero.

17
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.368
Target is just the reward — twenty.

18
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.779
Alpha times TD error: zero-point-one times twenty equals two.

19
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.965
The Dropoff bar rises to two-point-zero.

20
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.885
One step back: the target becomes zero-point-nine. The signal spreads.

21
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.392
Same transition — SARSA and Q-learning diverge.

22
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.349
SARSA samples the next action from the behavior policy.

23
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.499
Say it picks West: target is minus four-point-eight.

24
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.264
Q-learning ignores the sampled action entirely.

25
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.200
It asks: what is the best Q in the next state?

26
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.565
Target: plus zero-point-nine. Same data, opposite conclusion.

27
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.715
Off-policy means the behavior can be anything — the target stays greedy.

28
00:02:33.795 --> 00:02:37.656
R is the goal. The first dropoff gave it value two.

29
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.757
One step away: target zero-point-nine. Two steps: point-eight-five.

30
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.605
Values decay by gamma each hop — point-nine-five.

31
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.520
The greedy policy arrows emerge — no map was given.

32
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.224
Greedy episode: navigate to Y, Pickup, drive north to R.

33
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.437
Dropoff. Plus twenty. Four sub-goals, zero explicit planner.

34
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.989
Every arrow is the argmax of a converged Q-value row.

35
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.096
The boxed term — max of Q next-state — is the off-policy hook.

36
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.267
In the exercise: best_next equals max of Q of next_state.

37
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.200
That is the entire algorithmic difference from SARSA.

38
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.755
SARSA needs a sampled next action. Q-learning does not.

39
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.715
Q-learning is a sampled Bellman optimality backup — no model needed.

40
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.989
This exact update, scaled to a neural network, is DQN.
